Use io and string.

Define function digit_at taking text as a pointer to TcString, index as an unsigned number returning a whole number:
    Give back text.data[index] as a whole number - 48.
Done.

Let first be str_from_bytes(bytes_ptr("123"), bytes_len("123")).
Let second be str_from_bytes(bytes_ptr("456"), bytes_len("456")).
Let first length be length of address of first.
Let second length be length of address of second.
Let digits be an empty list of whole numbers.
For every i from 0 as an unsigned number to first length + second length - 1:
    push 0 to address of digits.

For every i from 0 as an unsigned number to first length - 1:
    Let a be digit_at(address of first, first length - 1 - i).
    For every j from 0 as an unsigned number to second length - 1:
        Let b be digit_at(address of second, second length - 1 - j).
        Let slot be i + j.
        Let total be list_get(address of digits, slot) + a * b.
        list_set(address of digits, slot, total modulo 10).
        Let carry be total divided by 10.
        Repeat while carry is more than 0:
            Add 1 to slot.
            Let next total be list_get(address of digits, slot) + carry.
            list_set(address of digits, slot, next total modulo 10).
            Set carry to next total divided by 10.

Let buffer be block of 64 byte.
Let written start at 0 as an unsigned number.
Let started be false.
For every step from 0 as an unsigned number to first length + second length - 1:
    Let index be first length + second length - 1 - step.
    Let digit be list_get(address of digits, index).
    If digit isn't 0 or started is true:
        Set item written of buffer to 48 + digit as a byte.
        Add 1 to written.
        Set started to true.
If started is false:
    Set item written of buffer to 48.
    Add 1 to written.
Let answer be create text from address of item 0 of buffer of length written.
Say answer.

str_free(address of answer).
list_free(address of digits).
str_free(address of second).
str_free(address of first).
Return 0.
